## 22. Antigravity playbook

> I have not verified Antigravity's current feature set. Adapt the mechanics below to whatever its current UI offers (project rules, agent instructions, task lists, artifacts). The principles hold regardless.

### 22.1 Setup
1. Put this `README.md` at the repo root and add a short `AGENTS.md` (or the tool's equivalent rules file) that says: *"Read README.md §2 (principles) and §22 before any task. Work on one phase at a time. Never invent data or APIs. Ask when unsure."*
2. Give the agent **one phase at a time**, never "build the whole app".
3. For each phase: have it **plan first** (files to create, tests to write), approve the plan, then implement, then run tests and lints, then summarise what changed and what is unverified.
4. Review every diff yourself. Commit per task. Merge only when CI is green.

### 22.2 Standing instructions for the agent
- Follow the structure in §7 and the contracts in §12–§13 exactly; propose changes via an ADR, don't drift silently.
- Write tests with the code. A task is not done until tests pass.
- Before using any library, read the installed version's docs or source; pin versions.
- If data is missing, create a `TODO(data)` and stop; do not fabricate numbers, rates, metrics or citations.
- Prefer small, reviewable PRs (≤ ~400 changed lines).
- After each task, report: what was done, how it was verified, what remains uncertain.

### 22.3 Phase prompts (copy-paste starters)

**Phase 0**
> Read README.md fully. Do not write code yet. Produce a plan to create the monorepo skeleton from §7: tooling (Ruff, mypy, ESLint, Prettier, pre-commit), docker-compose with Postgres, Makefile targets, `.env.example`, and a GitHub Actions workflow that lints and tests empty projects. List any assumptions as questions. Wait for approval.

**Phase 1**
> Implement the frontend per §15 against a mock API (MSW or a typed stub). Build design tokens, layout, landing, auth screens, the 4-step wizard, and the results page using placeholder data clearly marked as mock. Use Indian number formatting, accessible charts, loading/empty/error states. Add Vitest tests and an axe check. Do not build the real backend.

**Phase 2**
> Implement the backend per §12–§14: SQLAlchemy models and Alembic migrations, auth with Argon2id and rotating refresh tokens with reuse detection, projects CRUD with object-level authorisation, `/meta/*`, error envelope, rate limiting, audit log, health endpoints. Export OpenAPI and generate the frontend client. Write integration tests including IDOR and token-reuse cases. Do not implement ML yet; stub the estimation service behind an interface.

**Phase 3 (ML)**
> Implement the ML pipeline in `ml/` per §8–§9. First build ingest/validate/version for the data I supply (do not generate or invent rows; if I ask for a rate-derived generator, implement it transparently with `data_type` labels and documented assumptions). Then baselines, models, quantile intervals, confidence function, evaluation on the real-project holdout, model card, and registry. Report all metrics exactly as produced. Then implement breakdown and material engines per §10–§11 with property tests, and wire `POST /projects/{id}/estimates`.

**Phase 4**
> Complete the results dashboard charts and comparison view, implement the PDF service per §16 with a text-extraction test, finish accessibility/performance/security items in §14.2 and §17, add the Playwright E2E suite, and prepare production deployment and docs per §18. Produce a report against the §21 checklist with evidence for each item.

### 22.4 Red flags (stop and review if the agent does any of these)
Hard-coding sample costs into production code; claiming accuracy without a script output; adding packages without explaining why; disabling tests or type checks to get green; storing tokens in localStorage; building anything in the out-of-scope list; changing the DB schema without a migration.

